&lt;h1 class="relative group">Paper Summary: Retrieval-Augmented Generation with Conflicting Evidence
&lt;div id="paper-summary-retrieval-augmented-generation-with-conflicting-evidence" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#paper-summary-retrieval-augmented-generation-with-conflicting-evidence" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>&lt;a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2504.13079" target="_blank">arXiv Paper&lt;/a>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>A retrieval system reads documents, then writes an answer. This paper studies what happens when those documents disagree.&lt;/p>
&lt;div class="mermaid td-compact" align="center">
&lt;pre>
%%{init: {"flowchart": {"rankSpacing": 18, "wrappingWidth": 480}}}%%
flowchart TD
A["Retrieve documents"]
B["They conflict"]
C["Agents debate"]
D["One answer"]
A --> B --> C --> D
&lt;/pre>
&lt;/div>
&lt;p>MADAM-RAG gives each retrieved document its own agent, lets those agents debate, and uses an aggregator to write one answer. RAMDocs is the test set that mixes an unclear question, wrong documents, and documents that do not help.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>LLM Internal Encoding of Truthfulness and Hallucinations</title><link>https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/ps-llm-internal-encoding-of-truthfulness-and-hallucinations/</link><pubDate>Tue, 15 Apr 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>hari@dasarpai.com (Dr. Hari Thapliyaal)</author><guid>https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/ps-llm-internal-encoding-of-truthfulness-and-hallucinations/</guid><description>&lt;p>
&lt;figure>
&lt;img class="my-0 rounded-md" loading="lazy" src="https://dasarpai.github.io/assets/images/dspost/dsp6260-LLM-Internal-Encoding-of-Truthfulness-and-Hallucinations.jpg" alt="LLM Internal Encoding of Truthfulness and Hallucinations" />
&lt;/figure>
&lt;/p>
&lt;h1 class="relative group">Paper Summary: LLM Internal Encoding of Truthfulness and Hallucinations
&lt;div id="paper-summary-llm-internal-encoding-of-truthfulness-and-hallucinations" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#paper-summary-llm-internal-encoding-of-truthfulness-and-hallucinations" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>The objective of this paper is to gain a deeper understanding of errors produced by large language models (LLMs) by examining their internal representations. The authors aim to reveal how information about the truthfulness of LLM outputs is encoded internally, going beyond extrinsic, behavioral analysis. They also seek to investigate the relationship between these internal representations and the external behavior of LLMs, including their tendency to produce inaccuracies or &amp;ldquo;hallucinations&amp;rdquo;. Furthermore, the paper intends to explore whether internal representations can be used to predict the types of errors LLMs make and to detect the correct answer even when the model generates an incorrect one.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Book Review - Data Science in Marketing Analysis</title><link>https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/bookreview-data-science-for-marketing-analytics/</link><pubDate>Wed, 26 Oct 2022 15:50:00 +0530</pubDate><author>hari@dasarpai.com (Dr. Hari Thapliyaal)</author><guid>https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/bookreview-data-science-for-marketing-analytics/</guid><description>&lt;p>&lt;a href="https://dasarpai.github.io/assets/images/dspost/dsp6014-BookReview-Data-Science-for-Marketing-Analytics.jpg">Book Review - Data Science in Marketing Analysis&lt;/a>&lt;/p>
